Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=3 x^{\frac{2}{3}} - 1, \\f'(x)&=\frac{2}{x^{\frac{1}{3}}}=\frac{2}{\sqrt[3]{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(1)&=\frac{2}{\sqrt[3]{(1)}}=2, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{(2)}=- \frac{1}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(1,2)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(2)&=- \frac{1}{2}(x-(1)), \\y&=- \frac{x}{2} + \frac{5}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=- \frac{1}{2}\cdot 0+\left(\frac{5}{2}\right), \\y&=\frac{5}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(0,\frac{5}{2}\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- x^{\frac{1}{2}} - 1, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{1}{2 x^{\frac{1}{2}}}=- \frac{1}{2 \sqrt{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(1)&=- \frac{1}{2 \sqrt{(1)}}=- \frac{1}{2}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{1}{2}\right)}=2.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(1,-2)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-2)&=2(x-(1)), \\y&=2 x - 4.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=2x+(-4), \\x&=2.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(2,0)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=x^{\frac{3}{2}} - 3, \\f'(x)&=\frac{3 x^{\frac{1}{2}}}{2}=\frac{3 \sqrt{x}}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(4)&=\frac{3 \sqrt{(4)}}{2}=3, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{(3)}=- \frac{1}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(4,5)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(5)&=- \frac{1}{3}(x-(4)), \\y&=- \frac{x}{3} + \frac{19}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=- \frac{1}{3}\cdot 0+\left(\frac{19}{3}\right), \\y&=\frac{19}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(0,\frac{19}{3}\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- 3 x^{\frac{1}{4}}, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{3}{4 x^{\frac{3}{4}}}=- \frac{3}{4 x^{\frac{3}{4}}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(16)&=- \frac{3}{4 (16)^{\frac{3}{4}}}=- \frac{3}{32}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{3}{32}\right)}=\frac{32}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(16,-6)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-6)&=\frac{32}{3}(x-(16)), \\y&=\frac{32 x}{3} - \frac{530}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=\frac{32}{3}x+\left(- \frac{530}{3}\right), \\x&=\frac{265}{16}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(\frac{265}{16},0\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=-1 + \frac{1}{x}, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{1}{x^{2}}=- \frac{1}{x^{2}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(2)&=- \frac{1}{(2)^{2}}=- \frac{1}{4}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{1}{4}\right)}=4.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $\left(2,- \frac{1}{2}\right)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(- \frac{1}{2}\right)&=4(x-(2)), \\y&=4 x - \frac{17}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=4x+\left(- \frac{17}{2}\right), \\x&=\frac{17}{8}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(\frac{17}{8},0\right)$.
